addRun("a-second-judge-and-a-frontier-writer--writer-claude-haiku-5-5--harvey-labs--claude-code--environmental-esg-analyze-counterparty-markup-of-administrative-settlement-agreement", {"meta": {"benchmark": "harvey-labs", "harness": "claude-code", "model": "qwen3.8-27b", "task_title": "Analyze Counterparty Markup of Administrative Settlement Agreement — Redline Analysis Memorandum", "axis": "minute", "set": "a-second-judge-and-a-frontier-writer", "arm": "writer-claude-haiku-5-5"}, "events": [{"id": "event_001", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_002", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_003", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_004", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_005", "status": "unasked"}, {"id": "event_006", "status": "unasked"}, {"id": "event_007", "status": "unasked"}, {"id": "event_008", "status": "unasked"}, {"id": "event_009", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_010", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_011", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_012", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_013", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_014", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_015", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_016", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_017", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_018", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_019", "status": "unasked"}, {"id": "event_020", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_021", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_022", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_023", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_024", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_025", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_026", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_027", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_028", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_029", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_030", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_031", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_032", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_033", "status": "unasked"}, {"id": "event_034", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_035", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_036", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_037", "status": "unasked"}, {"id": "event_039", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_038", "status": "unasked"}, {"id": "event_040", "status": "unasked"}, {"id": "event_041", "status": "unasked"}], "questions": [{"id": "question_01", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The question asks what the tooling probe (which pandoc python3 libreoffice soffice plus a python3 import docx check) established, and how the agent translated that into a recorded plan. Card_0002 shows the probe exited 1 with only /usr/bin/pandoc and /usr/local/bin/python3 found and ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'docx'; card_0003 shows the agent recorded the result in process/session-note.md (pandoc available; python-docx NOT installed; plan to pandoc each docx to markdown, diff the redline, and build a pandoc-readable .docx memo with a status checklist). The report contains none of this: it never mentions pandoc, python, python-docx, libreoffice/soffice, the probe command, or session-note.md (grep counts are zero for each; the single 'docx' hit is only \"6 .docx files\" in the workflow diagram). The closest gesture is the Section 3 workflow diagram (source documents -> tracked-change extraction -> section-by-section analysis), which describes the later analysis pipeline but never states what the probe found or where the plan was recorded. The report-only reader's NOT_IN_REPORT is correct.", "evidence": ["card_0002", "card_0003"]}, {"id": "question_02", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The gold answer (per card_0005 and card_0006) is that pandoc failed on kensington-allocation-summary.docx with \"openBinaryFile: does not exist (No such file or directory)\" because the file is actually stored as kensington-allocation-summary.pdf.docx, and the agent recovered by converting the .pdf.docx separately in the next step. The report (a single HTML file) never mentions pandoc, the failed filename, the error, or the separate conversion step. It only gestures at the extraction topic in the workflow diagram (\"Source documents\", \"6 .docx files\", \"Tracked-change extraction\"), which does not materially answer which file failed, why, or how it was fixed. The report-only reader's NOT_IN_REPORT extraction is correct.", "evidence": ["card_0005", "card_0006"]}, {"id": "question_03", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The report (single file sample_report.html) never explains why the agent unzipped word/document.xml from lakeview-redline-asaoc.docx despite the pandoc markdown extract, nor what that led it to do. The gold answer rests on card_0007 (unzip -p ... | grep -o 'w:ins \\|w:del ' returning 63 w:ins and 52 w:del), card_0008 (decision to \"parse the tracked changes directly from the XML\"), and card_0012 (parsing \"to see exactly what is marked as inserted/deleted, with author and date\"). The report only gestures at the topic in its workflow diagram box \"Tracked-change\" / \"extraction\" / \"115 runs (Part I)\" (115 = 63 + 52, so consistent with the trace, but the figure appears with no explanation of the reason for going to the XML or the decision it drove) and the caption \"How the memorandum was built, from source documents through to the response strategy.\" That is a vague topic mention that does not materially answer the question; no mention of word/document.xml, unzip, pandoc, the 63/52 counts, or author/date attributes appears anywhere in the report. The report-only reader's NOT_IN_REPORT is correct.", "evidence": ["card_0007", "card_0008", "card_0012"]}, {"id": "question_04", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The report is a financial-impact summary of the redline memorandum (scenario dollar shares, key dollar impacts, and a workflow diagram). It never addresses how the agent handled the non-public HSR filing / potential Lakeview asset-sale intelligence in the written memo — no mention of the HSR filing, the asset sale, Caroline Thornfield's May 14 email instruction, verbal-only communication, discoverability, or any \"no HSR/sale references\" constraint — nor the legal basis for analyzing the Section 16.2/XVI contribution-protection expansion (no 113(f)(2), no successor-liability discussion, no \"hypothetical if-framing + disclaimer\" note for that scenario). The only near-gesture is the orphan-share bullet labeled \"Orphan-share exposure (hypothetical)\", but that concerns a defaulting Respondent's unfunded share, not the M&A scenario, so it does not materially answer the question. The report-only reader's NOT_IN_REPORT extraction is correct.", "evidence": ["card_0017", "card_0018", "card_0024"]}, {"id": "question_05", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The question asks how the agent treated the cover letter's claimed \"47 tracked changes\" and what count it actually established. Per card_0017 (the internal email's first pass: \"I count 47 tracked changes\") and card_0024 (session note: parsed word/document.xml, 115 runs / 63 ins / 52 del, reconciled to ~56 discrete edit locations, ~30 substantive, ~13 clerical), the trace-grounded answer is that the agent did not accept 47 at face value; it re-counted from the XML and reconciled the claim to ~56 edit locations. The report does not give this. The only \"47\" occurrences in the report are the $47.3M cost-basis figures, unrelated to tracked changes. The only tracked-change mention at all is a workflow-diagram node: \"Tracked-change / extraction / 115 runs (Part I)\" — a vague topic gesture that states a raw run count with no reference to the claimed 47, the decision to re-count, the insertion/deletion split, or the ~56-location reconciliation. That does not materially answer either half of the question (treatment of the claim; established count), so the reader's NOT_IN_REPORT stands and the report is scored missing.", "evidence": ["card_0017", "card_0024"]}, {"id": "question_06", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The question asks which key legal findings the agent locked in after reviewing the seven source documents (material breach of MRG §5.3 for the unilateral 27.6%→21.0% allocation rewrite, lapse/waiver of the §5.4 60-day dispute window from the Feb 10, 2024 Kensington report, and Kensington's prior rejection of Lakeview's allocation arguments in its Dec 20, 2023 comments) and what it did next (appending those findings to process/session-note.md before moving to drafting the memorandum). The report contains none of this. It presents only financial-impact scenarios (dollar shares, the +$3,484,800 allocation shift, cost basis, financial assurance, schedule) and a workflow diagram of how the memorandum was built (\"Source documents ... 6 .docx files\" through \"Response strategy (Part VIII)\"). The allocation percentages appear solely as financial scenario inputs, never framed as a §5.3 breach; the only MRG clause cited in the report is § 6.3 for the orphan-share exposure, which is unrelated to the locked-in legal anchors. Nowhere does the report mention a breach, a lapsed/waived dispute window, Kensington's prior rejection of the allocation arguments, or documenting findings in session-note.md as the next step. I searched the single-file report for \"5.3\", \"5.4\", \"breach\", \"waiv\", \"lapsed\", \"rejected\", \"session-note\", and \"drafting\" and found no supporting passage. The report-only reader's NOT_IN_REPORT extraction is correct.", "evidence": ["card_0023", "card_0024"]}, {"id": "question_07", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The report is a financial-impact summary of the memorandum's Part V figures. It never states the memo's identity (FROM James Petrucci / TO Caroline S. Thornfield, dated May 22, 2025), never explains why May 22 was chosen (to match the 5/22 full-draft review calendared in the email chain and sit before the 5/30 deadline), and never describes the markdown → pandoc → docx production plan with round-trip verification and a python-docx open check. Greps of the report source for \"pandoc\", \"Petrucci\", \"Thornfield\", \"2025\", \"deadline\", \"round-trip/round\", and \"verify\" return no matches. The only gestures are a passing reference to \"the redline analysis memorandum\" and a workflow diagram (\"How the memorandum was built, from source documents through to the response strategy\") whose boxes cover analysis stages, not the memo's identity, date rationale, or conversion/verification pipeline. Card_0024 shows the agent fixing the memo header (TO/FROM/DATE, banner, DRAFT label) and card_0026 shows the produced memo opening with exactly that identity; none of that material appears in the report. The report-only reader's NOT_IN_REPORT extraction is correct.", "evidence": ["card_0024", "card_0026"]}, {"id": "question_08", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The question asks which MRG Agreement section the agent cited for the allocation-dispute process and why, given that the email chain said \"Section 7.2\" and the Kensington summary said \"5.6\". Per card_0024, the agent checked the actual MRG Agreement text, cited §5.4 (the exclusive dispute process provision), and decided to flag the conflicting citations in a discrepancies section rather than conform to either secondary source (card_0017 shows the email-chain source the agent read). The report (input/sample_report.html, a single file) never addresses this. It is a financial-impact summary covering dollar shares under four scenarios, key dollar impacts (allocation shift, cost basis, initial payment, the ¶ 7.4 financial-assurance step-down, schedule costs, and § 6.3 orphan-share exposure), and a workflow diagram. There is no mention of §5.4, no \"7.2\" or \"5.6\" section references (the only \"5.6\" hit is an SVG coordinate), no mention of a dispute process or dispute clause, and no discrepancies section. The report only cites \"¶ 7.4\" and \"§ 6.3\" for unrelated financial points, so a reader could not reconstruct the source-verified §5.4 citation decision or the flagged discrepancy. The report-only reader's NOT_IN_REPORT verdict is confirmed by direct search.", "evidence": ["card_0017", "card_0024"]}, {"id": "question_09", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The question asks what the agent decided about the specific wire instructions embedded in redline §9.3 (FRBNY ABA 021030004, account 68010727). Per card_0024 (turn 34), the agent's session note recorded the item under untracked deviations as \"9.3 embeds specific wire instructions (FRBNY ABA 021030004 acct 68010727) — verify with EPA,\" i.e., it was left deliberately open rather than resolved or adopted. I searched the report (input/sample_report.html) myself: it is a financial-impact summary covering allocation shares, dollar impacts, and a workflow diagram. It contains no mention of \"wire,\" \"FRBNY,\" \"ABA,\" either number, \"9.3\" as a section reference (the only \"9.3\" hits are coordinate fragments in SVG like \"19.3\"), \"verify,\" \"untracked,\" or \"deviation,\" and no open/unresolved-item note. The report neither answers the question nor gestures at it, so the answer is absent. The report-only reader's NOT_IN_REPORT extraction is confirmed correct.", "evidence": ["card_0024"]}, {"id": "question_10", "answer": "The agent began by working from the source documents: six .docx files, from which it performed tracked-change extraction (115 runs, Part I) before moving on to section-by-section analysis, financial impact, the recommendation matrix, and response strategy. The first memo part (Part I) was the tracked-change extraction, containing 115 runs.", "quote": "How the memorandum was built, from source documents through to the response strategy.", "read": "complete", "reads": 1, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The question asks how the agent began composing the deliverable (announcing \"Now writing the memorandum\" after closing the source-analysis phase, then writing Part 1 — header, scope, and executive summary — to process/memo-part1.md) and what that first part contained (P&C banner, memo block to Thornfield from Petrucci, seven documents reviewed, method paragraph on 115 tracked-change runs / ~56 edit locations, headline conclusions, and an executive summary led by the +$3,484,800 allocation shift), per card_0025 and card_0026. The report never describes how drafting began: no \"now writing\" announcement, no process/memo-part1.md, no header/scope/executive-summary description of Part 1. The only gesture is the workflow figure (<figcaption>How the memorandum was built, from source documents through to the response strategy.<\/figcaption>) whose nodes run \"Source documents / 6 .docx files\" -> \"Tracked-change extraction / 115 runs (Part I)\" -> \"Section-by-section analysis (Part IV)\" -> \"Financial impact (Part V)\" -> \"Recommendation matrix (Part II.D)\" -> \"Response strategy (Part VIII)\". That is an analysis-pipeline diagram, not an account of where memo drafting started or what the first memo part contained, so it does not materially answer the question. The report separately mentions the +$3,484,800 figure only as one of the \"Key dollar impacts\" drawn from Part V, not as the lead of the memo's executive summary. The report-only reader's answer (\"The first memo part (Part I) was the tracked-change extraction, containing 115 runs\") is an over-inference from the figure's \"115 runs (Part I)\" node label and is not supported as an answer to this question; in the trace the memo's Part I is \"Purpose, Scope, and Sources\" with the executive summary as Part II.", "evidence": ["card_0025", "card_0026"]}, {"id": "question_11", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The question asks how the agent obtained redline-analysis-memorandum.docx despite the assembly chain exiting 127, and why it treated the DOCX as successfully produced. Per card_0034, the gold answer is that each chained step ran in order: cat assembled memo-full.md (15,371 words), pandoc completed and wrote the 55,290-byte DOCX, ls -la confirmed it, and only the trailing `file` command failed (\"command not found\") because the `file` utility was missing — which produced exit code 127, so the DOCX itself was produced successfully. The report is a single 9,915-byte financial impact summary (scenario chart, key dollar impacts, and a workflow figure) and never mentions exit code 127, pandoc, the memo assembly chain, `file: command not found`, or the ls -la verification of the DOCX. Its only adjacent material is the \"Analysis workflow\" section, whose figure caption says only \"How the memorandum was built, from source documents through to the response strategy\" — a vague gesture at the memo's construction that does not address the 127 failure or why the DOCX was produced. I searched the file for \"127\", \"pandoc\", \"exit\", \"command not found\", \"memo-full\", and \"assembl\" with no matches. The report-only reader's NOT_IN_REPORT extraction is correct.", "evidence": ["card_0034"]}, {"id": "question_12", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The question asks how the agent actually built redline-analysis-memorandum.docx from the drafted memo parts and what really caused the reported exit-127 failure. Per card_0033 and card_0034, the agent announced \"Now assemble and convert to .docx\" and ran a single chained command: cat of memo-part1..4.md into process/memo-full.md (15,371 words), pandoc conversion to redline-analysis-memorandum.docx (55,290 bytes), ls -la, and a final `file` call; the chain exited 127 only because the `file` utility was not installed (\"file: command not found\"), so the deliverable had in fact been produced and the failure was cosmetic.\n\nI searched the report (the single-file report at input/sample_report.html) verbatim: it contains no mention of \"127\", \"pandoc\", \"cat\", \"memo-part\", \"memo-full\", \"command not found\", \"file\", or \"exit\". It is a financial impact summary (scenario shares, key dollar impacts) plus a high-level \"Analysis workflow\" figure that traces the analysis stages from source documents through the recommendation matrix to response strategy. That diagram gestures at how the memorandum was produced as an analysis pipeline, but it never describes the docx assembly step (concatenating the four memo part files and converting with pandoc) and never addresses the reported failure or its cause (the missing `file` utility). The report-only reader's NOT_IN_REPORT verdict is correct.", "evidence": ["card_0033", "card_0034"]}, {"id": "question_13", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The gold answer is that the agent re-converted redline-analysis-memorandum.docx to markdown with pandoc (process/verify-roundtrip.md, 618 lines) and grepped the roundtripped text for the memo's five key dollar figures ($47,300,000, $52,800,000, $3,484,800, $21,542,400, $11,088,000), all of which were present, so the content-integrity check passed. The report never describes any such verification of the rendered DOCX: there is no mention of pandoc, a roundtrip, verify-roundtrip.md, rendered-file checking, or a pass result. The report does contain the dollar figures themselves (in its scenario table and \"Key dollar impacts\" list), but only as the memo's content, not as an account of how the rendered memo was checked — a topic mention that does not answer the question. The report-only reader's NOT_IN_REPORT verdict is confirmed.", "evidence": ["card_0036"]}, {"id": "question_14", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The report (a single file, input/sample_report.html) is a financial-impact summary: scenario dollar-share charts and tables, key dollar-impact bullets, and a workflow diagram. It contains no account of the round-trip verification finding two missing analyses, no mention of the redline's new Section II recitals (¶¶ 2.1–2.11) added to memo-part2.md, no mention of Section XXIII — General Provisions added to memo-part3.md, no account of the re-lettering (T→U, U→V), and no account of the grep -n \"Part IV\\.[A-Z]\" sweep and the cross-reference correction (Part IV.T → Part IV.S). Searches for round-trip, XXIII, re-letter, memo-part, cross-reference, grep, recital, missing, and verification all return no matches. The only gesture toward the topic is the generic workflow-diagram node \"Section-by-section\\nanalysis (Part IV)\", which states that Part IV of the memo contains section-by-section analysis but says nothing about the two missing sections, where they were added, or the lettering/cross-reference fallout, so it does not materially answer the question. The report-only reader's NOT_IN_REPORT answer is correct.", "evidence": ["card_0040", "card_0050", "card_0051", "card_0052", "card_0053"]}, {"id": "question_15", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The report (a single-file HTML summary of the redline's financial impact) never addresses the chunk-7 repair pass or any before/after corrections. It states the corrected figures as standing analysis — e.g. \"On the $47.3M base the shift is $3,121,800\" and the table rows showing $16,176,600 → $19,298,400 / $13,054,800 → $9,933,000 — but gives no \"before\" values ($1,190,400, \"about 25 days,\" \"a 30-day slip costs $45,000,\" the unsupported $500/day comparison) and no account of the agent discovering and fixing them during the repair pass, which is what the question asks. The report-only reader's NOT_IN_REPORT extraction matches what the report actually contains.", "evidence": ["card_0045", "card_0046", "card_0054", "card_0055"]}, {"id": "question_16", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The report is a single-file financial impact summary (scenario shares table, bar chart, key dollar impacts, and a high-level workflow diagram). It never mentions the chunk-7 repair pass or any of the corrections the gold answer rests on: the memo header date change May 22 -> May 16, 2025 (card_0038), the Section 5.4(a) deadline change April 11 -> April 10, 2024 plus the \"has not initiated\"/Kensington PRP rewording (card_0039), the $52.8M sentence re-citation from \"Draft ¶ 6(u) and ¶ 36\" to \"Draft ¶ 36\" with the added Draft ¶ 19 expansion (card_0042), the \"Draft's ¶ 6(t)\" -> \"¶ 6(u)\" structure fix (card_0043), or the garbled wire-instruction wording cleanup in memo-part3.md (card_0044). The only memo mentions are the generic \"Part V of the redline analysis memorandum\" note and the workflow figure caption about how the memorandum was built, neither of which addresses the repair pass. A search for repair, chunk, corrected, April 10/11, May 16/22, routing, ABA, and memo-part turns up nothing. The report-only reader's NOT_IN_REPORT extraction is accurate.", "evidence": ["card_0038", "card_0039", "card_0042", "card_0043", "card_0044"]}, {"id": "question_17", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The question asks what defect the first Edit call introduced when updating the NRD-reservation cross-reference (Part IV.T -> Part IV.U) in memo-part3.md, and how the second Edit resolved it. Per card_0047, card_0048, and card_0049, the first edit appended the \"deletion of the NRD reservation is compounded...\" sentence (with the corrected Part IV.U reference) after the Risk/Recommendation line while an earlier copy with the stale (Part IV.T) reference remained, duplicating the sentence; the agent noticed (\"I need to remove a duplicated sentence that my edit introduced, and update the cross-reference\") and the second edit deleted the duplicated copy, leaving one sentence with the corrected reference. The report contains no account of this: it is a financial-impact summary (scenario shares, cost basis, financial assurance, a workflow diagram) and mentions memo Part IV only as a workflow box (\"Section-by-section analysis (Part IV)\"), never the cross-reference update, the duplicated sentence, or either Edit call. A search of the report source for NRD, IV.T, IV.U, duplicate, cross-reference, memo-part, and edit turns up nothing. The report-only reader's NOT_IN_REPORT is correct.", "evidence": ["card_0047", "card_0048", "card_0049"]}, {"id": "question_18", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The question asks for the two consistency edits the agent made between the summary recommendation matrix and Part VI before the pre-proofread rebuild (per card_0058's announcement, card_0059's High→Critical escalation of matrix row 12 for ¶ 5.6, and card_0060's rewrite of consolidated item (7) in Part VI to cite ¶ 5.6 and ¶ 18.3 separately with the Part IV.O force-majeure fallback). I searched the full report (a single 9,915-byte HTML file): it contains only a financial-impact summary — a four-scenario dollar-share chart and table, a \"Key dollar impacts\" list, and a workflow diagram. The only \"Part VI\" match in the file is a substring of \"Part VIII\" in the workflow SVG. The report never mentions the consistency edits, the matrix row for ¶ 5.6, its risk rating, or the force-majeure consolidation in Part VI. The report-only reader's NOT_IN_REPORT extraction is correct; the answer is absent.", "evidence": ["card_0058", "card_0059", "card_0060"]}, {"id": "question_19", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The question asks what exactly failed in the scripted content check after the docx rebuild, why it failed (pandoc rendering artifacts: bold \"**DATE:**\", escaped \"\\$\", em-dash as \"---\"), and how the agent resolved it (confirmed the content was actually present — \"DATE: May 16, 2025\" line 9, Section XXIII at lines 545/547, \"60-day slip costs $45,000\" at line 585 — and moved on to structural verification). The report is a financial-impact summary (scenario table, dollar impacts, workflow diagram) and contains none of this. A case-insensitive grep for FAILED, pandoc, May 16, XXIII, 60-day, 45,000, round-trip/roundtrip, rendering, em-dash, and artifact finds zero matches. The report only gestures generally at how the memo was built (a \"How the memorandum was built\" workflow figure), which does not answer the verification-failure question. The report-only reader's NOT_IN_REPORT extraction is correct.", "evidence": ["card_0061", "card_0062", "card_0063", "card_0064"]}, {"id": "question_20", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The report is a single-file \"Lakeview Redline Analysis: Financial Impact Summary\" covering scenario dollar shares, key dollar impacts, and a generic \"Analysis workflow\" diagram. I searched the report source for the material terms (pandoc, round-trip/roundtrip, heading, newline, blank line, memo-part, memo-full, Section IX, escaped); none appear. The report does not explain why the \"H. Section IX\" heading vanished from the round-tripped docx (part2's final line joining part3's heading line in the cat concatenation, producing escaped inline text per cards 0065-0068), does not mention the first hypothesis that the part files were missing trailing newlines and the od -c check that disproved it (cards 0069-0070), and does not describe the actual fix of appending blank lines to parts 1-3 (cards 0071-0072). Its only workflow diagram gestures at how the memorandum was built, not at this failure/recovery. The report-only reader's NOT_IN_REPORT extraction is correct.", "evidence": ["card_0065", "card_0066", "card_0067", "card_0068", "card_0069", "card_0070", "card_0071", "card_0072"]}, {"id": "question_21", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The question asks how the agent verified that the blank-line fix restored the \"H. Section IX\" heading in the deliverable. Per card_0073, the agent re-concatenated the four parts to memo-full.md, regenerated redline-analysis-memorandum.docx with pandoc, round-tripped it back to process/verify-roundtrip.md, and checked the heading structure: 34 level-1/2 headings, \"## G. Section VIII\" at line 320, and the restored \"## H. Section IX --- Payments to EPA Special Account\" at line 324. The report (sample_report.html, a single ~9.9KB file) is a financial impact summary (scenario charts, dollar-impact bullets, a workflow diagram) and never mentions the blank-line fix, pandoc regeneration, the round-trip file, heading counts, or the \"H. Section IX\" heading; searches for these terms return nothing. The closest content is the generic \"Analysis workflow\" diagram, which shows how the memorandum was built, not how the heading repair was verified on a rebuilt deliverable. The report-only reader's NOT_IN_REPORT extraction is correct.", "evidence": ["card_0073"]}, {"id": "question_22", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The question asks what the agent did after restoring the missing heading: per card_0074 it declared a \"full final proofread of the rendered document,\" and per card_0075 it conducted that step by reading the ~634-line pandoc round-trip file process/verify-roundtrip.md in windows (sed -n '1,110p'), i.e. proofing the rendered output rather than re-reading the memo source parts. The report contains none of this. It is a single-file financial-impact summary (scenarios, dollar shares, and a workflow diagram), and the only process-adjacent content is the workflow figure (\"Source documents → Tracked-change extraction → Section-by-section analysis (Part IV) → Financial impact (Part V) → Recommendation matrix (Part II.D) → Response strategy (Part VIII)\"), which describes the memo's content pipeline, not any post-repair proofreading pass. A search of the report source finds no mention of \"proofread,\" \"heading,\" \"restored,\" \"render,\" \"round-trip,\" or \"verify-roundtrip.\" The report-only reader's NOT_IN_REPORT is correct.", "evidence": ["card_0074", "card_0075"]}, {"id": "question_23", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The report is a single HTML file that only presents the financial impact summary: a scenario bar chart, a key-dollar-impacts list, and a workflow diagram of how the memorandum was built. It never mentions the final proofread, the duplicated \"no PRP had invoked the dispute procedures\" sentence found in item 2 of Part II.A, or the edit to memo-part1.md that deleted the redundant sentence. A search of the report text for proofread, redundant/duplicate, PRP, invoked, dispute, Kensington noted, and memo-part1 returns no matches, so the trace-grounded answer (cards 0075-0077: the agent spotted the repeated sentence and deleted the second occurrence via Edit on memo-part1.md) is absent. The report's \"Analysis workflow\" section gestures at process generally but does not cover the proofread pass or any content-level fix, so this is a topic-level mention at best, not an answer.", "evidence": ["card_0075", "card_0076", "card_0077"]}, {"id": "question_24", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The gold answer is a specific late proofread edit: after reading lines 210-360 of process/verify-roundtrip.md, the agent changed the IV.C.2 \"Risk: High. Recommendation: Counter.\" line in process/memo-part2.md from \"(Draft ¶¶ 6(u), 36)\" to \"(Draft ¶ 36; see also ¶ 19)\" while retaining the $52,800,000 Total Estimated Cost / financial-assurance base — the \"IV.C.2 citation fix\" later picked up by the rebuilt docx (card_0079: sed -n '210,360p' process/verify-roundtrip.md; card_0080: the Edit with exactly that old/new string; card_0083: \"rebuild the docx to pick up the two late edits (Part II.A de-duplication and the IV.C.2 citation fix)\"). The report is a single-file financial-impact summary (scenario chart, dollar-impact bullets, workflow diagram) and contains no account of this proofread edit: no mention of IV.C.2 as an edited item, of the citation correction, of proofreading, of memo-part2.md edits, or of the late edit/rebuild. The only nearby gestures are structural labels in the workflow diagram (\"Section-by-section analysis (Part IV)\", \"6 .docx files\"), which describe the memo's architecture, not what was changed. A vague structural mention does not materially answer the question, so this is missing_from_report. The report-only reader's NOT_IN_REPORT is corroborated.", "evidence": ["card_0079", "card_0080", "card_0083"]}, {"id": "question_25", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The question asks how the agent concluded the full-document proofread (rendered text through Part VII clean, then tail of verify-roundtrip.md checked — 634 lines all clean) and what it decided to do next (rebuild the docx to pick up the two late edits — Part II.A de-duplication and the IV.C.2 citation fix — before running the full validation suite), per cards 0083–0085. The report contains none of this. It is a financial-impact summary of the Lakeview redline (scenario table, dollar impacts, and a build-workflow diagram); a case-insensitive search of the report source finds no occurrences of proofread, round-trip, 634, tail, rebuild, validation, or the \"--- JPM\" signature. The only process-adjacent content is the \"3. Analysis workflow\" figure with the caption \"How the memorandum was built, from source documents through to the response strategy,\" which describes how the memo was assembled (source documents → tracked-change extraction → section-by-section analysis → financial impact → recommendation matrix → response strategy); it is a vague topic mention of the workflow that does not address the proofread conclusion or the rebuild-before-validation decision. The report-only reader's NOT_IN_REPORT answer is correct.", "evidence": ["card_0083", "card_0084", "card_0085"]}, {"id": "question_26", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The question asks which structural verifications were run against the rebuilt redline-analysis-memorandum.docx and what each showed. Per card_0087 the agent ran a Python zipfile + python-docx script (zip OK, 16 parts, no corrupt member, 291 non-empty paragraphs, 4 tables shaped 7x4/4x3/32x5/5x4), and per card_0088 it round-tripped the docx through pandoc to process/verify-roundtrip.md (634 lines); both checks passed. I searched the entire report (the single staged file, sample_report.html) for every material term — zipfile, pandoc, round-trip/roundtrip, verify, 634, 291, paragraphs, parts, corrupt, valid, structural — and none appear. The report covers only the financial content (scenario share chart and table, key dollar impacts) and a generic \"Analysis workflow\" figure showing source documents -> tracked-change extraction -> section-by-section analysis -> financial impact -> recommendation matrix -> response strategy; the closest it comes to the topic is that workflow caption, \"How the memorandum was built, from source documents through to the response strategy,\" which never names any verification of the rebuilt docx or its results. The report-only reader's NOT_IN_REPORT extraction is correct.", "evidence": ["card_0087", "card_0088"]}, {"id": "question_27", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The report-only reader's NOT_IN_REPORT is correct. The report (a single sample_report.html) covers only the memo's financial content: scenario dollar-share charts/tables, key dollar impacts, and a high-level \"Analysis workflow\" diagram (source documents -> tracked-change extraction -> section analysis -> financial impact -> recommendation -> strategy). A search for verification-related terms (grep, verif, assert, round-trip, passed, check) finds nothing. It never describes the final grep verification suite against process/verify-roundtrip.md — the must-be-present key figures (~38 strings like $52,800,000, CERCLA-05-2025-0037, wire IDs, \"63 insertions, 52 deletions\"), the two latest edits (duplicated \"no PRP had invoked\" sentence count of exactly 1 and the \"Draft ¶ 36; see also ¶ 19\" IV.C.2 citation fix), the ten must-be-absent terms (HSR, Hart-Scott, Rodino, asset sale, stale values 1,190,400 and 02100004, \"about 25 days\", \"DATE: May 22\", \"May 22, 2025\", \"\\\\##\") — or that all assertions passed, as cards 0089-0091 show. The report's only gesture toward process is the workflow diagram's \"Tracked-change extraction / 115 runs (Part I)\" box, which is about memo construction, not final content verification, so the answer is absent rather than merely vague.", "evidence": ["card_0089", "card_0090", "card_0091"]}, {"id": "question_28", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The report is a financial impact summary (scenario shares for Hartwell and Lakeview, key dollar impacts, and a workflow diagram) and never addresses the final spot-checks. It does not mention the O&M grep returning empty, the number-format mismatch between \"$4.3 million\"-style patterns and the full-numeric \"$4,300,000\" figures in the Crestline memo, the recovery via \"pump-and-treat\"/\"MNA\" re-searches and the sed read of Sections 4.3-4.5, or any of the confirmed figures ($4,300,000 P&T O&M of $16,300,000; $2,100,000 MNA sampling of $5,100,000; $1,800,000 institutional controls; TCE 4,800 μg/L = 960x MCL; 1.2-mile plume; 12 extraction wells; Q3 2025 signed-ASAOC target), matching what card_0092-card_0095 show. The only related wording in the report is the incidental phrase \"before O&amp;M and escalation\" inside a bullet about schedule cost, which gestures at O&amp;M as a cost category without answering any part of the question, so this is a vague topic mention, not an answer. The report-only reader's NOT_IN_REPORT extraction is correct.", "evidence": ["card_0092", "card_0093", "card_0094", "card_0095"]}, {"id": "question_29", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The gold answer (cards 0095, 0098) is that the agent appended a \"### Final verification log (2026-10-05 session close-out)\" section to process/session-note.md containing the post-first-build corrections and the final build/check results, ending by marking \"Deliverable: /app/workspace/redline-analysis-memorandum.docx — DONE\" (note left at 77 lines). The report is a one-page financial impact summary: a scenario share chart and table, a key dollar impacts list, and a build workflow diagram. It never mentions process/session-note.md, any appended verification log, the close-out section, or the DONE marking. A grep for session/close/DONE/verif/log/append/record turns up only CSS class names and SVG marker attributes, not content. The only process-adjacent gesture is the workflow figure (\"How the memorandum was built, from source documents through to the response strategy.\"), which describes the analysis pipeline, not the formal session close-out, so it does not materially answer the question. The report-only reader's NOT_IN_REPORT is correct.", "evidence": ["card_0095", "card_0098"]}], "stages": {"spend": {"census": {"sessions": 0, "seconds": 0}, "questions": {"sessions": 0, "seconds": 0}, "answer": {"sessions": 57, "seconds": 5531}, "judge": {"sessions": 29, "seconds": 7592}, "claims": {"sessions": 18, "seconds": 6010}, "recoveries": {"sessions": 5, "seconds": 567}}, "claims": [{"id": "c1", "claim": "The report was delivered as an HTML financial-impact summary (\"Lakeview Redline Analysis: Financial Impact Summary\") of the redline analysis memorandum, marked Draft, and its scenario figures are stated as analytical rather than predictions of EPA's position.", "type": "outcome_claim", "section": null, "verdict": "unsupported_or_contradicted", "why": "The claim says the report was \"delivered as an HTML financial-impact summary ('Lakeview Redline Analysis: Financial Impact Summary')\". The cards contradict the format and framing: card_0100 (terminal) states the deliverable is `/app/workspace/redline-analysis-memorandum.docx`, a full memorandum (Parts I–VIII, DRAFT status, TO Caroline S. Thornfield / FROM James Petrucci) whose Part V is the financial-impact table — not an HTML document and not a standalone financial-impact summary titled \"Lakeview Redline Analysis: Financial Impact Summary\". card_0099 lists the workspace and contains only .docx files; no .html file appears in the entire 100-card trace (grepped for html/doctype/text.html). Th … [352 more characters]"}, {"id": "c2", "claim": "Under the \"Draft (Kensington allocation)\" scenario on the $52.8M cost base, Hartwell's dollar share is $18,057,600 (34.2%) and Lakeview's is $14,572,800 (27.6%).", "type": "other", "section": null, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0075 shows the memorandum's table \"Against the Crestline Estimate of $52,800,000, the dollar effect is:\" with row \"Kensington Allocation (Draft)  $18,057,600  $14,572,800\" for Hartwell/Lakeview, and the adjacent percentage table shows Hartwell 34.2% and Lakeview 27.6% — exactly matching the claim's figures and scenario. card_0078 (same document, lines 110-210) confirms the draft's allocation row \"3.3, 4.1, App. B | Allocation 27.6/34...\", corroborating the 34.2%/27.6% draft (Kensington) percentages."}, {"id": "c3", "claim": "Under the \"Lakeview allocation only\" scenario on the $52.8M cost base, Hartwell's dollar share is $21,542,400 (40.8%) and Lakeview's is $11,088,000 (21.0%).", "type": "other", "section": null, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0032 shows the Part V Financial Impact Summary table written into memo-part4.md, whose \"Lakeview allocation only ($52.8M)\" row lists exactly $52,800,000 total base, Hartwell $21,542,400 (40.8%), and Lakeview $11,088,000 (21.0%) — matching the claim verbatim, including the arithmetic (40.8% and 21.0% of $52,800,000 = $21,542,400 and $11,088,000). card_0090's verification grep confirms \"21,542,400\" (3 hits) and \"11,088,000\" (5 hits) are present in the final rendered docx roundtrip."}, {"id": "c4", "claim": "Under the \"Cost basis only (Kensington allocation)\" scenario on the $47.3M cost base, Hartwell's dollar share is $16,176,600 (34.2%) and Lakeview's is $13,054,800 (27.6%).", "type": "other", "section": null, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0032 shows the memo's Part V \"Financial Impact Summary\" table written with the exact row the claim quotes: \"Cost basis only (Kensington Allocation; $47.3M) | $47,300,000 | $16,176,600 (34.2%) | $13,054,800 (27.6%)\", i.e., Hartwell $16,176,600 (34.2%) and Lakeview $13,054,800 (27.6%) on the $47.3M base under the Kensington allocation. card_0090 shows the final report's round-trip verification grepping the built document and finding \"16,176,600\" (2 occurrences), \"13,054,800\" (3), and \"47,300,000\" (5), confirming the figures landed in the delivered memorandum."}, {"id": "c5", "claim": "Under the \"Redline as a whole (Lakeview allocation)\" scenario on the $47.3M cost base, Hartwell's dollar share is $19,298,400 (40.8%) and Lakeview's is $9,933,000 (21.0%).", "type": "other", "section": null, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0032 (write of memo-part4.md) contains the Financial Impact Summary table with the row \"Redline as a whole (Lakeview allocation; $47.3M) | $47,300,000 | $19,298,400 (40.8%) | $9,933,000 (21.0%)\", matching the claimed scenario, cost base, and both shares exactly. The arithmetic is consistent (40.8% × $47.3M = $19,298,400; 21.0% × $47.3M = $9,933,000), and card_0090's verification grep confirms these strings survived into the final document."}, {"id": "c6", "claim": "The scenario comparisons use Crestline's $52.8M figure or EPA's preliminary $47.3M figure as the cost base, paired with either Kensington's draft allocation or Lakeview's redline allocation.", "type": "other", "section": null, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0032 shows the Part V \"Financial Impact Summary\" scenario table with exactly four rows: Draft (Kensington Allocation; Crestline $52.8M), Lakeview allocation only ($52.8M), Cost basis only (Kensington Allocation; $47.3M), and Redline as a whole (Lakeview allocation; $47.3M). The accompanying text also identifies the bases as \"Crestline $52.8M vs. EPA preliminary $47.3M\", matching the claim's labels. card_0100 corroborates that the final memo's Part V contains a \"four-scenario cost table\". The scenarios therefore use the $52.8M or $47.3M base paired with either the Kensington draft or the Lakeview redline allocation, as stated."}, {"id": "c7", "claim": "Shifting from Kensington's draft allocation to Lakeview's redline allocation moves +$3,484,800 to Hartwell and −$3,484,800 from Lakeview on the $52.8M base, with the shift equal to $3,121,800 on the $47.3M base.", "type": "other", "section": null, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0020 shows Kensington's draft allocation of Hartwell 34.2% ($18,057,600) and Lakeview 27.6% ($14,572,800), which implies the $52.8M base. card_0024 records the redline's allocation change as 27.6→21.0 (Lakeview) / 34.2→40.8 (Hartwell) and the base as $52.8M→$47.3M — a ±6.6-point shift from Lakeview to Hartwell. 6.6% of $52.8M is $3,484,800 and 6.6% of $47.3M is $3,121,800, exactly the claimed shift, and card_0090's post-build grep confirms both figures appear in the final deliverable next to the four per-PRP dollar allocations (21,542,400/18,057,600/11,088,000/14,572,800), whose differences match $3,484,800. card_0098's close-out log independently records the corrected figure \"Allocatio … [72 more characters]"}, {"id": "c8", "claim": "The cost basis change (from $52.8M to $47.3M) is −$5,500,000 in total, and Crestline states that the lower base leaves a $5.5M financial assurance gap.", "type": "other", "section": null, "verdict": "supported", "why": "The Crestline memo (read in full via card_0019) states Crestline's total estimated cost is $52,800,000, approximately $5,500,000 (11.6%) higher than EPA's preliminary estimate of $47,300,000, and expressly says (its Section 7/8 discussion) that \"The use of the lower EPA estimate ($47,300,000) would create a financial assurance gap of $5,500,000 that could leave the remediation underfunded.\" Card_0024's session notes corroborate the $52.8M vs $47.3M figures and the $5.5M differential, and card_0100's close-out summary lists \"the $5.5M assurance gap\" among the dollar impacts. The arithmetic ($52.8M − $47.3M = $5.5M) and the attribution to Crestline both check out."}, {"id": "c9", "claim": "The initial payment decreases by $550,000 (from $5,280,000 to $4,730,000), at 10% of the respective bases.", "type": "other", "section": null, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0030 (the memo's Section IX analysis) states the tracked change \"Initial payment $5,280,000 → $4,730,000\" and explains it is conforming to the cost-basis counter: on the $52.8M base the 10% initial payment is $5,280,000, and the $550,000 reduction flows entirely from the cost-basis change (10% of $47.3M = $4,730,000). card_0032 and card_0055 show the executive-summary line written into the memo: \"Initial payment: −$550,000 ($5,280,000 → $4,730,000), 10% of the respective bases.\" card_0090 confirms the final document contains \"550,000\", \"5,280,000\", \"4,730,000\", \"52,800,000\", and \"47,300,000\". The arithmetic and the claim's statement both match the saved evidence."}, {"id": "c10", "claim": "Lakeview's illustrative financial assurance decreases by $4,639,800 on the combined redline (from $14,572,800 to $9,933,000), and the automatic 15% annual step-down in ¶ 7.4 reduces assurance to 44.4% of the original after five years and 19.7% after ten.", "type": "other", "section": null, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0055's structured patch preserves the memo's financial-impact list, item 4: \"Financial assurance (Lakeview, illustrative): −$4,639,800 on the combined redline ($14,572,800 → $9,933,000). Layered on top, the automatic 15% annual step-down (¶ 7.4) reduces assurance to 44.4% of its original amount after five years and 19.7% after ten, regardless of work performed\" — the claim restates this passage exactly. The underlying figures are corroborated: card_0024's session-note analysis records the redline's \"15%/yr FA step-down regardless of work (7.4)\" and the allocation/cost-basis changes behind the two assurance amounts (27.6% × $52.8M = $14,572,800; 21.0% × $47.3M = $9,933,000; difference $4 … [236 more characters]"}, {"id": "c11", "claim": "The schedule change carries about $996,000 in direct incremental project-management cost (12 × $33,000 RD phase + 12 × $50,000 RA phase), before O&M and escalation.", "type": "other", "section": null, "verdict": "supported", "why": "The full text of the Crestline cost-estimate memo (read in card_0019, Section 4.6) states the incremental project-management cost is \"approximately $33,000 per additional month during the Remedial Design phase and $50,000 per additional month during the Remedial Action phase.\" Card_0024's session note records the derived figure: \"PM sensitivity +$33K/mo RD, +$50K/mo RA → +24 mo ≈ $996K direct.\" The final memo text (card_0079 and card_0082) states it as claimed: \"roughly $996,000 of additional project-management cost for a 12+12 month extension, before long-term O&M and escalation,\" and the impact summary itemizes \"12 × $33,000/month RD phase + 12 × $50,000/month RA phase,\" which is exactly 1 … [103 more characters]"}, {"id": "c12", "claim": "Hypothetical orphan-share exposure is $11–15 million for the group if a defaulting Respondent's share goes unfunded.", "type": "other", "section": null, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0024 (session note) records the analysis: \"orphan share $11.088M–$14.5728M,\" and card_0084 shows the final memo text stating the orphan-share exposure (hypothetical) if a defaulting Respondent's share goes unfunded is $11,088,000–$14,572,800 on the $52.8M base, \"i.e., an $11–15 million exposure for the group,\" with the MRG §6.3 pro-rata pass-through context. card_0100's close-out summary likewise lists the \"$11–15M orphan-share exposure\" as a delivered financial impact. The claim matches the source-derived figures and the hypothetical framing."}, {"id": "c13", "claim": "Under MRG Agreement § 6.3, Hartwell would absorb about $6.88M of the orphan-share exposure on the Kensington-basis figure, raising its effective exposure to about $24.9M.", "type": "other", "section": null, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0022 (full read of the MRG governance agreement excerpt) shows § 6.3 \"Default and Orphan Share Allocation\": a Defaulting Member's share is assumed pro rata by Non-Defaulting Members per their Allocated Shares, and Schedule A shows Hartwell's 34.2% share. card_0024 records the orphan-share exposure as $11.088M–$14.5728M (on the $52.8M Kensington-basis estimate). card_0084 shows the agent's memo stating exactly the claimed analysis: on the Kensington-basis figure, Hartwell's additional absorption ≈ $6.88M (34.2/72.4 × $14,572,800), raising its effective exposure to ≈ $24.9M ($18.0576M own share + $6.88M ≈ $24.94M). card_0090 confirms the final roundtrip verification found \"6.88 million\" a … [109 more characters]"}, {"id": "c14", "claim": "The analysis proceeded in phases from source documents through section-by-section analysis (Part IV), financial impact (Part V), recommendation matrix (Part II.D), and on to the response strategy (Part VIII).", "type": "task_transition", "section": null, "verdict": "supported", "why": "The cards show the analysis proceeding in the claimed phases. card_0024 records the source-document phase (parsed tracked changes, untracked deviations, source-verified legal anchors from the draft ASAOC, redline, cover letter, Crestline, Kensington, MRG agreement, and email chain). The index rows for card_0030 and card_0032 show the Part IV section-by-section content and the Part V \"Financial Impact Summary\" written after that source-analysis phase, and card_0032 confirms the Part V write. card_0065 shows the built document's heading order: \"II. Executive Summary\" with \"D. Summary recommendation matrix\" (Part II.D), then \"IV. Section-by-Section Analysis\", matching the claimed part labels. c … [239 more characters]"}, {"id": "c15", "claim": "A tracked-change extraction was performed and yielded 115 runs (Part I of the memorandum).", "type": "task_transition", "section": null, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0013 shows the python3 script that parses word/document.xml of the Lakeview redline and its output: \"Total tracked change runs: 115\" (63 ins / 52 del per the w:ins/w:del counts in card_0007, all author=\"Author\"). card_0026 shows Part I of the memorandum (\"I. Purpose, Scope, and Sources\", written to process/memo-part1.md) stating the method: \"We extracted and categorized all 115 tracked-change runs in the file (63 insertions, 52 deletions)\". The extraction was performed and the 115-run figure is what Part I reports, exactly as the claim states."}, {"id": "c16", "claim": "The source documents for the analysis were 6 .docx files.", "type": "other", "section": null, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0001 (find/ls of /app/workspace) shows the source material is exactly six .docx files — crestline-cost-estimate-memo.docx, kensington-allocation-summary.pdf.docx, lakeview-cover-letter.docx, lakeview-redline-asaoc.docx, mrg-governance-agreement-excerpt.docx, thornfield-draft-asaoc.docx — plus one non-.docx source (thornfield-internal-email-chain.eml). card_0005 shows the agent extracting all six .docx sources to markdown (the sixth, mrg-governance-agreement-excerpt, succeeded; kensington-allocation-summary.pdf.docx was handled separately). card_0099's final ls confirms the same six Jun-23-timestamped .docx source files (the seventh .docx, redline-analysis-memorandum.docx, is the agent's … [207 more characters]"}], "recoveries": [{"event": "event_001", "verdict": "adapted", "why": "card_0002 is the failing probe: `python3 -c \"import docx\"` exited 1 with\nModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'docx' (tool reported failure). The next\nattempt at the same goal — obtaining docx parsing capability — changed the\napproach in two ways relevant to the failure: card_0004 fixes the reported\ncause directly with `pip install python-docx`, and card_0005 switches the\nextraction approach to pandoc (`pandoc \"$f.docx\" -t markdown ...`), which is\nthe tool the probe confirmed was available. The agent never re-ran the failing\nimport check with no change."}, {"event": "event_003", "verdict": "adapted", "why": "card_0005 is the failing batch pandoc extraction: five .docx files converted OK, but kensington-allocation-summary.docx failed with \"openBinaryFile: does not exist\". In the next chunk, card_0006 retries the same conversion with the correct filename kensington-allocation-summary.pdf.docx (the actual file, already listed in the earlier find output of card_0001) and it succeeds (OK). The retry changed the input path to fix the exact cause the failure reported."}, {"event": "event_007", "verdict": "adapted", "why": "card_0016 is the failing Read of /app/workspace/process/extracts/thornfield-internal-email-chain.eml, which returned \"File does not exist\" (the .eml was never extracted into process/extracts). Immediately after, card_0017 retries the same read with a different path, /app/workspace/thornfield-internal-email-chain.eml, which succeeds and returns the email chain content. Changing the path is a relevant change that fixes the reported cause, so the agent adapted before its next attempt."}, {"event": "event_018", "verdict": "adapted", "why": "card_0034 is the failing assembly command (cat of the four memo parts, wc, pandoc to docx, ls, file) that exited 127 because `file: command not found`. The next attempt at verifying the docx (card_0035) drops the missing `file` utility and instead validates the file with python3: a zipfile check for [Content_Types].xml and word/document.xml plus opening the docx with python-docx, which succeeds (16 parts, 282 paragraphs, 4 tables). The verification approach changed in a way directly relevant to the reported cause."}, {"event": "event_037", "verdict": "adapted", "why": "card_0096 is the failing command: `tail -n 30 process/session-note.md` run without cd'ing into /app/workspace, so the relative path failed to resolve (exit 1, \"No such file or directory\"). card_0097 is the immediate retry of the same goal (tail + wc -l on process/session-note.md) prefixed with `cd /app/workspace &&`, which fixes the exact cause the failure reported — the relative path — and it succeeds, printing the note's contents and the line count. The next attempt changed the working directory/path resolution, which is directly relevant to the failure, so this is an adaptation rather than a bare re-run."}]}, "branches": [], "reports": {"html": "<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"utf-8\">\n<title>Lakeview Redline Analysis: Financial Impact Summary<\/title>\n<style>\n  body { font-family: system-ui, -apple-system, \"Segoe UI\", Arial, sans-serif; color: #1a1a1a; max-width: 960px; margin: 32px auto; padding: 0 20px; line-height: 1.5; }\n  h1 { font-size: 1.6rem; margin: 4px 0 8px; }\n  h2 { font-size: 1.15rem; margin-top: 2.2rem; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; padding-bottom: 4px; }\n  .banner { font-size: .78rem; letter-spacing: .04em; text-transform: uppercase; color: #7a1f1f; }\n  .note { font-size: .88rem; color: #555; }\n  table { border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; font-size: .92rem; margin-top: 10px; }\n  th, td { border-bottom: 1px solid #e3e3e3; padding: 6px 8px; text-align: right; }\n  th:first-child, td:first-child { text-align: left; }\n  thead th { border-bottom: 2px solid #999; }\n  figure { margin: 16px 0; }\n  figcaption { font-size: .85rem; color: #555; margin-top: 6px; }\n  svg { max-width: 100%; height: auto; display: block; }\n  ul { padding-left: 20px; }\n  li { margin-bottom: 6px; }\n<\/style>\n<\/head>\n<body>\n<div class=\"banner\">Privileged &amp; Confidential · Attorney Work Product · Draft<\/div>\n<h1>Lakeview Redline Analysis: Financial Impact Summary<\/h1>\n<p class=\"note\">Dollar shares for Hartwell Chemical and Lakeview Metallurgical under four scenarios, from Part V of the redline analysis memorandum. Scenario figures are analytical and are not predictions of EPA's position.<\/p>\n\n<h2>1. Shares under four scenarios<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<svg data-pil-kind=\"plot\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 720 390\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Bar chart of Hartwell and Lakeview dollar shares under four scenarios\"><line x1=\"70\" x2=\"700\" y1=\"310.0\" y2=\"310.0\" stroke=\"#e5e5e5\"/><text x=\"62\" y=\"314.0\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#555\">$0<\/text><line x1=\"70\" x2=\"700\" y1=\"256.0\" y2=\"256.0\" stroke=\"#e5e5e5\"/><text x=\"62\" y=\"260.0\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#555\">$5M<\/text><line x1=\"70\" x2=\"700\" y1=\"202.0\" y2=\"202.0\" stroke=\"#e5e5e5\"/><text x=\"62\" y=\"206.0\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#555\">$10M<\/text><line x1=\"70\" x2=\"700\" y1=\"148.0\" y2=\"148.0\" stroke=\"#e5e5e5\"/><text x=\"62\" y=\"152.0\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#555\">$15M<\/text><line x1=\"70\" x2=\"700\" y1=\"94.0\" y2=\"94.0\" stroke=\"#e5e5e5\"/><text x=\"62\" y=\"98.0\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#555\">$20M<\/text><line x1=\"70\" x2=\"700\" y1=\"40.0\" y2=\"40.0\" stroke=\"#e5e5e5\"/><text x=\"62\" y=\"44.0\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#555\">$25M<\/text><rect x=\"96.8\" y=\"115.0\" width=\"48\" height=\"195.0\" fill=\"#1f4e79\"/><text x=\"120.8\" y=\"109.0\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#222\">$18.1M<\/text><rect x=\"152.8\" y=\"152.6\" width=\"48\" height=\"157.4\" fill=\"#c9a227\"/><text x=\"176.8\" y=\"146.6\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#222\">$14.6M<\/text><text x=\"148.8\" y=\"328\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#222\">Draft<\/text><text x=\"148.8\" y=\"344\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#555\">Kensington · $52.8M<\/text><rect x=\"254.2\" y=\"77.3\" width=\"48\" height=\"232.7\" fill=\"#1f4e79\"/><text x=\"278.2\" y=\"71.3\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#222\">$21.5M<\/text><rect x=\"310.2\" y=\"190.2\" width=\"48\" height=\"119.8\" fill=\"#c9a227\"/><text x=\"334.2\" y=\"184.2\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#222\">$11.1M<\/text><text x=\"306.2\" y=\"328\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#222\">Lakeview allocation only<\/text><text x=\"306.2\" y=\"344\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#555\">Lakeview · $52.8M<\/text><rect x=\"411.8\" y=\"135.3\" width=\"48\" height=\"174.7\" fill=\"#1f4e79\"/><text x=\"435.8\" y=\"129.3\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#222\">$16.2M<\/text><rect x=\"467.8\" y=\"169.0\" width=\"48\" height=\"141.0\" fill=\"#c9a227\"/><text x=\"491.8\" y=\"163.0\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#222\">$13.1M<\/text><text x=\"463.8\" y=\"328\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#222\">Cost basis only<\/text><text x=\"463.8\" y=\"344\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#555\">Kensington · $47.3M<\/text><rect x=\"569.2\" y=\"101.6\" width=\"48\" height=\"208.4\" fill=\"#1f4e79\"/><text x=\"593.2\" y=\"95.6\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#222\">$19.3M<\/text><rect x=\"625.2\" y=\"202.7\" width=\"48\" height=\"107.3\" fill=\"#c9a227\"/><text x=\"649.2\" y=\"196.7\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#222\">$9.9M<\/text><text x=\"621.2\" y=\"328\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#222\">Redline as a whole<\/text><text x=\"621.2\" y=\"344\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#555\">Lakeview · $47.3M<\/text><rect x=\"70\" y=\"12\" width=\"12\" height=\"12\" fill=\"#1f4e79\"/><text x=\"88\" y=\"22\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#222\">Hartwell Chemical<\/text><rect x=\"230\" y=\"12\" width=\"12\" height=\"12\" fill=\"#c9a227\"/><text x=\"248\" y=\"22\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#222\">Lakeview Metallurgical<\/text><text x=\"14\" y=\"175.0\" transform=\"rotate(-90 14 175.0)\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#555\">Dollar share<\/text><\/svg>\n<figcaption>Dollar share by scenario. Cost base is Crestline's $52.8M or EPA's preliminary $47.3M; allocation is Kensington's draft or Lakeview's redline.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Scenario<\/th><th>Cost base<\/th><th>Hartwell share<\/th><th>Lakeview share<\/th><\/tr><\/thead>\n<tbody><tr><td>Draft (Kensington allocation)<\/td><td>$52,800,000<\/td><td>$18,057,600 (34.2%)<\/td><td>$14,572,800 (27.6%)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Lakeview allocation only<\/td><td>$52,800,000<\/td><td>$21,542,400 (40.8%)<\/td><td>$11,088,000 (21.0%)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Cost basis only (Kensington allocation)<\/td><td>$47,300,000<\/td><td>$16,176,600 (34.2%)<\/td><td>$13,054,800 (27.6%)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Redline as a whole (Lakeview allocation)<\/td><td>$47,300,000<\/td><td>$19,298,400 (40.8%)<\/td><td>$9,933,000 (21.0%)<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n\n<h2>2. Key dollar impacts<\/h2>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Allocation shift:<\/strong> +$3,484,800 to Hartwell and −$3,484,800 from Lakeview on the $52.8M base. On the $47.3M base the shift is $3,121,800.<\/li>\n  <li><strong>Cost basis:<\/strong> −$5,500,000 in total ($52.8M to $47.3M). Crestline states the lower base leaves a $5.5M financial assurance gap.<\/li>\n  <li><strong>Initial payment:<\/strong> −$550,000 ($5,280,000 to $4,730,000), 10% of the respective bases.<\/li>\n  <li><strong>Financial assurance (Lakeview, illustrative):<\/strong> −$4,639,800 on the combined redline ($14,572,800 to $9,933,000). The automatic 15% annual step-down (¶ 7.4) reduces assurance to 44.4% of the original after five years and 19.7% after ten.<\/li>\n  <li><strong>Schedule:<\/strong> about $996,000 in direct incremental project-management cost (12 × $33,000 RD phase + 12 × $50,000 RA phase), before O&amp;M and escalation.<\/li>\n  <li><strong>Orphan-share exposure (hypothetical):<\/strong> $11–15 million for the group if a defaulting Respondent's share goes unfunded. Under MRG Agreement § 6.3, Hartwell would absorb about $6.88M of that on the Kensington-basis figure, raising its effective exposure to about $24.9M.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h2>3. Analysis workflow<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<svg data-pil-kind=\"diagram\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 900 110\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Workflow from source documents to response strategy\"><defs><marker id=\"arw\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"4\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z\" fill=\"#666\"/><\/marker><\/defs><rect x=\"1\" y=\"17\" width=\"138\" height=\"76\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#eef3f8\" stroke=\"#1f4e79\"/><text x=\"70.0\" y=\"51.0\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#222\" font-weight=\"600\">Source documents<\/text><text x=\"70.0\" y=\"67.0\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#222\">6 .docx files<\/text><line x1=\"141\" y1=\"55.0\" x2=\"151\" y2=\"55.0\" stroke=\"#666\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#arw)\"/><rect x=\"153\" y=\"17\" width=\"138\" height=\"76\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#eef3f8\" stroke=\"#1f4e79\"/><text x=\"222.0\" y=\"43.0\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#222\" font-weight=\"600\">Tracked-change<\/text><text x=\"222.0\" y=\"59.0\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#222\">extraction<\/text><text x=\"222.0\" y=\"75.0\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#222\">115 runs (Part I)<\/text><line x1=\"293\" y1=\"55.0\" x2=\"303\" y2=\"55.0\" stroke=\"#666\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#arw)\"/><rect x=\"305\" y=\"17\" width=\"138\" height=\"76\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#eef3f8\" stroke=\"#1f4e79\"/><text x=\"374.0\" y=\"51.0\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#222\" font-weight=\"600\">Section-by-section<\/text><text x=\"374.0\" y=\"67.0\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#222\">analysis (Part IV)<\/text><line x1=\"445\" y1=\"55.0\" x2=\"455\" y2=\"55.0\" stroke=\"#666\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#arw)\"/><rect x=\"457\" y=\"17\" width=\"138\" height=\"76\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#eef3f8\" stroke=\"#1f4e79\"/><text x=\"526.0\" y=\"51.0\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#222\" font-weight=\"600\">Financial impact<\/text><text x=\"526.0\" y=\"67.0\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#222\">(Part V)<\/text><line x1=\"597\" y1=\"55.0\" x2=\"607\" y2=\"55.0\" stroke=\"#666\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#arw)\"/><rect x=\"609\" y=\"17\" width=\"138\" height=\"76\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#eef3f8\" stroke=\"#1f4e79\"/><text x=\"678.0\" y=\"51.0\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#222\" font-weight=\"600\">Recommendation<\/text><text x=\"678.0\" y=\"67.0\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#222\">matrix (Part II.D)<\/text><line x1=\"749\" y1=\"55.0\" x2=\"759\" y2=\"55.0\" stroke=\"#666\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#arw)\"/><rect x=\"761\" y=\"17\" width=\"138\" height=\"76\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#eef3f8\" stroke=\"#1f4e79\"/><text x=\"830.0\" y=\"51.0\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#222\" font-weight=\"600\">Response strategy<\/text><text x=\"830.0\" y=\"67.0\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#222\">(Part VIII)<\/text><\/svg>\n<figcaption>How the memorandum was built, from source documents through to the response strategy.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<\/body>\n<\/html>\n"}, "same_run": "harvey-labs--claude-code--environmental-esg-analyze-counterparty-markup-of-administrative-settlement-agreement"});
